#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "ec516fb4-a497-579c-b19d-ebc0a081eb58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5822" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u}  =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( 3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u} & =\\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ 3u(u+1)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{u+1}-\\frac{1}{3u}) & =3u(u+1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u(u+1)}{u+1}-\\frac{3u(u+1)}{3u} & =\\frac{15u(u+1)}{u(u+1)} \\\\ \\frac{9u\\cdot \\cancel{(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3u}\\cdot (u+1)}{\\cancel{3u}} & =\\frac{15\\cdot \\cancel{u(u+1)}}{\\cancel{u(u+1)}} \\\\ 9u-(u+1) & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9u-(u+1) & =15 \\\\ 9u-u-1 & =15 \\\\ 8u & =16 \\\\ u & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\neq -1$ and $2\\neq 0,$ we conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/id #uuid "a578b895-ea0e-5d83-aa1d-04a2fe15f332" :question/math-academy-id "e-5824" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2}  = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big( k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2} & =\\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ (k-2)(k-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{k-3}-\\frac{2}{k-2}) & =(k-2)(k-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)(k-3)}{k-3}-\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{k-2} & =\\frac{2(k-2)(k-3)}{(k-2)(k-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(k-2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k-3)}}{\\cancel{k-3}}-\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)}\\cdot (k-3)}{\\cancel{k-2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}}{\\cancel{(k-2)(k-3)}} \\\\ 3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(k-2)-2(k-3) & =2 \\\\ 3k-6-2k+6 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "3384b27a-d977-5421-800e-64a3581b939d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9367" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ x(x-2)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{8}{x(x-2)}) & =x(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x(x-2)}{x-2}-\\frac{8x(x-2)}{x(x-2)} & =\\frac{3x(x-2)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4x\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x-2}}-\\frac{8\\cancel{x(x-2)}}{\\cancel{x(x-2)}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{x}(x-2)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 4x-8 & =3(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ 4x-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ 4x-3x & =-6+8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f621599-1486-51cc-8798-5435cea2df6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251461} {:answer/id #uuid "809d97c8-9929-5f4b-867e-e1e1c94fbe97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251635} {:answer/id #uuid "885d0314-5aa8-5885-b994-cc12bef28f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251702} {:answer/id #uuid "e6833253-d35d-5d86-b5bb-9455ea6698cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252570} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e43e56-3d56-5413-b69c-00d7701f6053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6833253-d35d-5d86-b5bb-9455ea6698cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "m=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252570} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef01d8ee-239f-5582-826d-a723a4095517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "237cb996-b5fc-51c0-b848-df3a338c9048" :question/math-academy-id "q-117687" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+4,\\,m-3,\\,(m+4)(m-3))=(m+4)(m-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-4$ and $m=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3} & =\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ (m+4)(m-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}) & =(m+4)(m-3)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{m+4}+\\frac{(m+4)(m-3)}{m-3} & =\\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)}(m-3)}{\\cancel{m+4}}+\\frac{(m+4)\\cancel{(m-3)}}{\\cancel{m-3}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}}{\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}} \\\\ 3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3 \\\\ 4m-5 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =8 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne -4$ and $2\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $m=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0801c677-febc-5048-b4a9-bd16e29096aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250620} {:answer/id #uuid "259a4b59-97e1-5b2a-ae19-ec0054e36543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250878} {:answer/id #uuid "9b65b064-032f-5a74-8b4d-b5fd5feeec61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251892} {:answer/id #uuid "def82426-7548-5608-afb8-c119d37b39c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252518} {:answer/id #uuid "f83cbd43-a1c5-5373-b93c-4ff388a4a312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def82426-7548-5608-afb8-c119d37b39c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252518} :answer-field/id #uuid "6942e994-071a-50b2-a350-0106e069e5db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "686ef7d4-296f-572a-a3ca-d6455dccd76c" :question/math-academy-id "q-118117" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}+4v}=\\frac{3}{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}=\\frac{3}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(v+4,\\,v(v+4),\\,v)=v(v+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $v=0$ and $v=-4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ v(v+4)\\cdot (\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}) & =v(v+4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v(v+4)}{v+4}+\\frac{12v(v+4)}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3v(v+4)}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v\\cdot \\cancel{(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v+4}}+\\frac{12\\cdot \\cancel{v(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v(v+4)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{v}\\cdot (v+4)}{\\cancel{v}} \\\\ 6v+12 & =3(v+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $v,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6v+12 & =3(v+4) \\\\ 6v+12 & =3v+12 \\\\ 3v & =0 \\\\ v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $v=0$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba949de-81f6-54a0-8f19-c9fed59db56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251051} {:answer/id #uuid "763eccac-cf6b-548f-9e91-96b068420c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251525} {:answer/id #uuid "85d973e6-d911-51db-a990-b05e52077b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251674} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5e0cde-460b-5ce5-b7b3-75cfdfb23cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252062} {:answer/id #uuid "f73b5a87-92e7-5417-92ca-a4a93b3aa7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba949de-81f6-54a0-8f19-c9fed59db56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251051} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5e003f-a698-53ab-98d2-6521927c967e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3e07973-1de0-5078-b548-53734ee05751" :question/math-academy-id "q-118123" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nWe make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z-1,\\,2z,\\,z(z-1))=2z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ 2z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}) & =2z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{10z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ 10z-10 & =z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10z-10 & =z-1 \\\\ 9z & =9 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c24c8638-bc45-58d8-92ca-8e5999196644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c24c8638-bc45-58d8-92ca-8e5999196644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291466} :answer-field/id #uuid "13cc09e6-8210-5c2c-84d3-0adddb80ebe7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5052ef42-8b3c-5bc4-ad50-88c2f81dda82" :question/math-academy-id "q-276459" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}=\\frac{2}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n+4,\\,n(n+4),\\,n)=n(n+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $n=-4$ and $n=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2}{n} \\\\ n(n+4)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}) & =n(n+4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n(n+4)}{n+4}-\\frac{5n(n+4)}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2n(n+4)}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n\\cdot \\cancel{(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n+4}}-\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{n(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n(n+4)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot (n+4)}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ 3n-5 & =2(n+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-5 & =2(n+4) \\\\ 3n-5 & =2n+8 \\\\ n & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $13\\ne 0$ and $13\\ne -4,$ we conclude that $n=13$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $n=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17043857-3227-572b-b4b7-6ed400421ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360933} {:answer/id #uuid "35f1c23e-4f90-5bc5-a18f-a8492ea035e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361079} {:answer/id #uuid "74d18717-fb29-5681-a346-e6ed7697370a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361351} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec7f463-3427-592f-90f0-c6c4b74d7bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361471} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bae244-6b93-50b4-a686-78c3894fb2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74d18717-fb29-5681-a346-e6ed7697370a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361351} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54d9c25-2457-5ba9-aa66-2f2a91622cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "305f4a20-a5d5-5769-aa7d-36e413111285" :question/math-academy-id "q-98699" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}=\\frac{1}{w-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,\\,w(w-3),\\,w-3)=w(w-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=0$ and $w=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)} & =\\frac{1}{w-3} \\\\ w(w-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}) & =w(w-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{w-3} \\\\ \\frac{2w(w-3)}{w}+\\frac{3w(w-3)}{w(w-3)} & =\\frac{w(w-3)}{w-3} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{w}(w-3)}{\\cancel{w}}+\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{w(w-3)}}{\\cancel{w(w-3)}} & =\\frac{w\\cdot \\cancel{(w-3)}}{\\cancel{w-3}} \\\\ 2w-6+3 & =w\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2w-6+3 & =w \\\\ w & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $w=3$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}]}
